        <div id="runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Long Does a Heated Vest Last? The Battery Decides, Not the Heat Zones
          </h2>

          <p>
            Heated vest listings lead with heat zones: four, six, fifteen, twenty-one. Zones tell you where the heat goes. They tell you almost nothing about how long it lasts, and for anyone working outside the second question is the one that matters. A vest that is toasty for ninety minutes and then goes cold halfway through clearing the driveway is worse than a plain fleece, because you dressed for the heat and now it is gone.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watt-Hours Are the Real Fuel Gauge</h3>

          <p>
            Every heated vest runs on a battery, and the honest measure of a battery is watt-hours: volts multiplied by amp-hours. A typical power-bank vest pack of 7.4 volts and 5,000mAh holds about 37 watt-hours. A Milwaukee M12 2.0Ah pack holds about 24 watt-hours, while a common 18V 5.0Ah tool battery holds about 90. More watt-hours means more hours of heat at the same setting. Zone count can actually work against runtime, because more heated area draws more power from the same pack.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Runtime Claims Are Low-Setting Numbers</h3>

          <p>
            When a listing says up to 10 or up to 20 hours, it means the lowest heat setting. High heat typically draws several times as much power as low, so the same vest might last a full day on low and a few hours on high. Plan around medium. If a vest is rated for 10 hours, expect roughly half that at a setting you can actually feel on a windy, sub-freezing morning.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cold Batteries Give Less</h3>

          <p>
            Lithium batteries deliver less energy when they are cold. Keep the pack in its inside pocket rather than clipped outside, store spares inside your coat or in the truck cab, and charge them indoors. A pack that starts the day warm will run noticeably longer than one pulled from a freezing garage shelf.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Real Fix: A Second Battery</h3>

          <p>
            The single best upgrade to any heated vest is a spare battery. For power-bank vests, that means buying a second pack from the same brand. For tool-platform vests, you probably already own several, which is the whole case for them.
          </p>
        </div>

